nd gravity and even the  presence of adjacent objects and  this is showing the light responsiveness  of the organism this  uh isolate on the left is a blind mutant  and the one on the right is responding  to a white  light source off the edge of the plate  and the sporangiopores reorient their  growth towards the light source  so when alex sidner was a postdoc in my  lab he was studying  light sensing and other properties in  cryptococcus but  his weekend hobby was to come in and  work on phycomyces he just fell in love  with this organism  and contributed a series  of publications on ficomyces  the first two were in collaboration with  louise corotrano  and arturo aslava's groups in spain and  involved identifying  the genes and the proteins involved in  light sensing  these are mutants that have been  isolated in max del brook's lab in the  60s mad a and mad b and they encode  orthologs of the white color one white  color two protein complex  that senses light and neurospora  alex went on to identify the mating type  locus  in this uh fungal species they it had  been earlier named  the sex locus these were studies  published in nature  will return to the sex locus as part of  the main story in just a few minutes  and then finally there was a large  consortium that came together  that was organized by louise corotrano  and victor garr and igor grigoria  who sequenced the genomes of both  phycomyces  and mucours cersei's this was published  in current biology just a few years ago  and what it revealed is that these  species have  undergone repeated whole genome  duplication events  we know about the whole genome  duplication event that punctuated the  evolution of saccharomyces cerevisiae  that's critical in producing  duplicated genes and paralogs with many  interesting biological functions  these species have undergone two whole  genome duplication events and  that turns out to be a recurrent theme  throughout biology and other  species as well and what one of the