sistant they  shut off expression of the key drug  target and then when they revert  magically it comes back on  so we considered a number of different  possible models for unstable drug  resistance and i want to focus  on the one that turned out to be the  explanation  we knew that mucor had an active rnai  pathway from work  going on in spain at the university of  mercia  in the labs of victor garr rosa ruiz  vasquez  and santiago torres martinez they and  their colleagues have been identifying  components of the rnai pathway including  dicer argonauts and rna-dependent rna  polymerases  contributing a series of publications on  this topic and  so we entertain the hypothesis that  these drug-resistant isolates might  involve spontaneous  rna silencing of the fkbp12 gene  to test that we made small rna  from the wild type the various forms of  the epi mutant grown in fk 506 in the  resistant form  or in the reverted sensitive form ran  them out and probed them  with a probe specific for antisense  rna to the fkbp12 gene encoded by the  fkba gene  and remarkably you'll see that all of  these epi-mutant isolates have abundant  small rnas to the fkbp12 locus but when  they revert to be sensitive  those all disappear we made  small rna libraries and sequence them so  we could look at the repertoire of small  rnas across the entire genome  and what this lower panel shows those  displayed across the fkvp12 gene  which has three exons the wild type has  no small rnas to the gene  the three different resistant epi  mutants all have abundant  smaller rnas to both the sense and  anti-sense strand  and when they revert those small rnas go  away  and notably you'll see that the introns  of the gene are not decorated by small  rnas  only the exons and so it implicates the  mature  mrna from the fkbp12 gene as being the  target  for the small rnas now importantly we  did not see any spreading of small rnas  to adjacent genes  we didn't see amplification of small  rnas  in the dr