ined the species  complex here for  mucours or sinaloides and we've been  talking about cersenoloides and  lucitanicus but  you can see by the ps designation which  means  phylogenetic species there's 16  different species in this complex  of which at least five or six are human  pathogens  so we're quite interested now we've set  up a collaboration with grit  in her lab to bring in isolates across  the species complex and test  the whole panel for formation of epi  mutations  now the other major advance in this  paper is  for many years you could do sexual  crosses in muchor but no one had ever  seen the spores germinate so you could  not do a complete  sexual cross and this is the first  report  of zygosport germination from some of  the isolates reported in their  phylogenetic tree  so we're also quite excited about that  because for the first time we can do a  cross  and see if that might change the  frequency of epi mutations  and it might link it to that helicase  that's right next to the mating type  locus i should mention that we've tested  a lot of other stress conditions  and with the exception of infection  which i'll tell you about in a few  minutes  a panoply of other stress conditions in  vitro didn't seem to stimulate the  formation of epi mutations  um my graduate student zanetta chang who  is an md phd student  then extended this in another direction  and that was to identify epi mutations  in two additional genes this relied on  using a drug  5-fluorouric acid 5foa that was  popularized by the yeast genetics  community  because it selects for loss of function  in the urethria or the ura5 genes  and in mucor these are referred to as  pref and pure g  we spent quite a bit of time looking for  five of five foa resistant epi mutants  unsuccessfully  and then zanetta um took a page from um  this the delineation of the rnai  pathways and she started  with those rnai mutants that have a  higher frequency of epi mutants  a kind of genetic helping hand so i