for all of this  support and  i i'd like to also highlight that this  is a program  that's now supported also by the  canadian institute for advanced research  called fungal kingdom  threats and opportunities i'll stop  there and be happy to take any questions  if there's time  thank you very much for a wonderful  seminar  thank you we have  a question from  eric and then uh from gillian  in the chat eric no thank you very much  so quick question so if you um look at  your epigenetic resistant  um organism and then essentially make an  si rna library  do you think that you're going to see  depletion of lots of different si rnas  you know is this like a sort of a global  phenomenon that many that you're having  a control of lots of the si rnas  or is it something specific to the  resistance of the  um drug that you're testing so  from the libraries that we've made for  the  fkbp 12 resistant mutants  and for the 5 foa resistant mutants  essentially all we see is small rnas  increasing in abundance to those target  genes  all the other small rnas that we see  across the genome  seem to be at the same level of  abundance in the  the wild type the resistant isolate and  the revert and isolate  so the way we're thinking about it is if  you look at a whole population of mucor  say a billion cells there'll be  a cell here that has more small rnas to  one  gene and there'll be a cell here that  has more small rnas to another gene  and a cell here that has small rnas  to another gene but uh  we're not sure that there would be an  intermediate that had say a lot of small  rnas across the genome  and we may be thinking about that in the  wrong way but  we're thinking of it similar to how  mutations occur  but it could also be the case when  you're growing a culture that you get  bursts of mutation as well  so for the ones that come out of the  brain  we've only analyzed those cfu as a whole  population  and we see seven genes that have an  increase  in small rnas and what we don't know  is does every cell coming out of the  brain  have small rnas increased against all  seven of those genes  or by analyzing it as a pool and we  combine  seven different epi mutants in our  analysis  so we want to repeat that experiment  and include colony purification as part  of it  to begin to address that  so this sounds like more like a  chromatin structure kind of change then  would that be your thought um we  actually  don't think there's going to be a change  in the chromatin structure  we've been um struggling with some of  the reagents to be able to prove that  definitively  um it's certainly possible that there's  some  heterochromitin but the fact that  the the trigger is the mature mrna  we think means that it might be  post-transcriptional and it might not  involve heterochromatin because  if you were to form heterochromatin you  you might imagine you wouldn't have any  rna there to continue the process  thanks  gillian you had a question that's in the  chat i see  is there any association or connection  of the epi mutation phenomenon  to the whole genome duplication that  that's a great question um  so we don't know and  what we so when we tried to generalize  this to other species we spent some time  trying to look for epi mutations in  neurospora and in cryptococcus  unsuccessfully and  we then went back and said let's look at  the closest species we can look at  so we are thinking about broadening that  now to these 16  phylogenetic species but they've all  undergone this whole genome duplication  event so  we need isolates that would undergo this  that are further away  and i think we're going to try harder  with cryptococcus and um  we've been hoping that somebody would  find this in neurosphere or aspergillus  in the meantime um  yeah you pre-empted my  second question um has this  been demonstrated for any plant pathogen  not that i'm aware of and  you know the silvia's paper was  published in  2014 and i thought there might be a  flurry  of reports that followed that  but um it it took some time  and robin alshires i think is really the  first example  there is another paper that i skipped  over at the end there  in the interest of time where they  characterize  fluconazole resistant cryptococcus  isolates and about half of them turn out  to be  aneuploid for one chromosome or another  but the other half  does they're unstable and they don't  have any aneuploidy  and they don't know what the mechanism  is  so we would like to make small rna  libraries of those isolates and see if  they  might be epi mutants and i think that  that could be a good  starting point i think for plant  pathogens  given robin allshire's paper you could  entertain the idea that they might be  either  heterochromatin or they might be rnai or  given the plant and the human work they  might be dna methylation  right so any any of these mechanisms  could give rise  to something that might be unstable at  some level  thank you any other questions  i have a question then then um so much  are famous for their resistance to  various anti-fungal agents um  do you think uh that there is a common  mechanism uh related to  your recent discoveries or these um  these um ability the ability to to  withstand  the the pressure is  by different mechanisms so  it's one of the defining features i  think of the mucouralies as a human  pathogen and  they are intrinsically resistant to a  lot of drugs  and when you look at rnai mutants that  couldn't  form an epi mutation they don't become  more sensitive  to those drugs  we think there are other reasons  in the case of some of the azoles basils  can have a short  or a medium or a long tail  and there are some amino acid  substitutions in erg 11 the target of  the azoles  that make much resistant to some azoles  but still sensitive to  things like isobuconazole  tobias hall was visiting one time and we  presented this story to him and he said  you know it's pretty interesting but it  would be a lot more interesting  if you could show this would be relevant  to a drug we actually use in people  and so we are planning to look for epi  mutants that might make you resistant to  amphotericin b  or poseiconazole or isobuconazole  and it turns out you can get loss of  function mutations in their  gastro biosynthetic pathway that could  do that so an  earth-six mutant should be amphotericin  b resistant and erg-free mutant or  epimutant  would be azoll resistant some of those  are  one of those genes is triplicated in  mucor so  that might be a unique case where it'd  be easier to get an epi mutant than a  loss of function mutation  so when carlos and maribel arrive  that's part of what we're planning to  pursue  and that might then link it to um  mechanisms of drug resistance there's a  lot of  drug resistance occurring i think in  both plant and human fungal pathogens  that's in response to azoll pressure  in agriculture this is a huge problem  with  aspergillus fumigatus and there's the  suspicion that it might be contributing  to  canada oris drug resistance  infections come from the environment so  we're also going to look at agricultural  azoles as well  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


